Chapter: CritterCheck
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Critter
  cites Judge chapter Grade
  cites Gold chapter CritterGold

 Grade Safari chapter Critter, which draws the animals' baked stills.

 A CHECK OF ITS OWN FOR A BUNDLE-SIZE REASON, not a logical one. The two generated
 tables are 216 KB and 534 KB of Codex literals; folded into RenderCheck on top of
 everything that check already cites, codexzig aborts outright -- not a diagnostic,
 a core dump. Apart they transpile comfortably. That is PORTING_NOTES C7 shaping
 the port's STRUCTURE rather than just a tolerance, for the second time.

 Both draw functions ARE pub, so both seams are real oracles: the probe calls the
 game's own function and reads the words it put on paint's wire.

 STRUCTURE EXACTLY, COORDINATES WITH THE PIXEL GATE. A still is baked art scaled by
 one number, so a wrong point count is a wrong polygon and every colour is a
 literal carried through unchanged; neither takes any tolerance.

 THE BULL IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT from the animal cases, and saying so is the
 honest part. It is the only Fluent COLOR animal and 40 of its 43 polygons carry a
 2-stop gradient, which paint.zig writes as wire tags 5 and 6. Safari chapter Paint
 models tags 0, 1 and 3, so the generated table flattens each gradient to its first
 stop and the port emits tag 0 where the game emits 5 or 6. Grading it would
 compare two different wire shapes and prove nothing. The seven FLAT species are
 exact, and the bull becomes gradeable the day Paint grows the two tags.

 THE CAT'S COORDINATES ARE SAMPLED EVERY THIRD POINT and that is C7 again: seven
 stills are 13,453 points, 26,906 Reals, and one Real literal past about 16,868
 exhausts the transpiler's bump heap. Point counts stay exact, so a still that
 gained or lost a polygon fails hard regardless; only the density is thinned.
 Mountains hit the same ceiling and took the same answer.

 THE CAT'S HOP IS WHAT ITS CASES ARE FOR. It is applied in the UNIT FRAME -- added
 to each polygon's own y before scaling -- so it is measured in cat-heights and
 shrinks with distance like the rest of the still. Reading it as a metres offset to
 the anchor would look nearly right up close and drift wrong as the cat recedes,
 which one near sample would wave through. Two of the seven cases are airborne, at
 different depths.

 We say:

Section: The Cases

  CritterCase = record {
    cp : Integer,
    h : Real,
    fwd : Real,
    face : Boolean
  }

  critter-cases : List CritterCase
  critter-cases = [CritterCase { cp = 129414, h = 0.9, fwd = 14.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 128024, h = 2.8, fwd = 40.0, face = True }, CritterCase { cp = 129426, h = 4.5, fwd = 90.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 129427, h = 1.6, fwd = 25.0, face = True }, CritterCase { cp = 129423, h = 2.2, fwd = 60.0, face = False }, CritterCase { cp = 128004, h = 1.4, fwd = 18.0, face = True }, CritterCase { cp = 128022, h = 1.1, fwd = 9.0, face = False }]

  critter-wire : Integer -> List DrawCmd
  critter-wire (i) =
    if i >= list-length critter-cases then []
    else (critter-draw 2.5 (list-at critter-cases i).fwd (list-at critter-cases i).h (list-at critter-cases i).cp (list-at critter-cases i).face focal camera-w) & critter-wire (i + 1)

Section: Flattening

  s-tags : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-tags (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).tag] & s-tags cs (i + 1)

  s-cols : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-cols (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).color] & s-cols cs (i + 1)

  s-counts : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  s-counts (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [list-length (list-at cs i).pts / 2] & s-counts cs (i + 1)

  s-coords : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  s-coords (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (list-at cs i).pts & s-coords cs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-tag " (s-tags (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-tag)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-col " (s-cols (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-col)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "cr-cnt " (s-counts (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-cnt)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "cr-xy  " (s-coords (critter-wire 0) 0) g-cr-xy 0.001 0.000001)
  end
